    "top_frame": "J.T. Ginn shut down Washington over 6.1 scoreless innings, and the offense did the rest early.",
    "what_this_means": "This team doesn't need drama. They took a 5-0 lead in the first inning. The game was decided before most lineups were even warm. Ginn's line was immaculate: one hit, seven strikeouts, nothing given. The bats stayed live through four more scoring innings. This is what happens when the pitcher is that clean and the lineup doesn't waste it. McNeil and Wilson drove in runs early, and the game became a formality by the sixth. The blowout absorbed what should have felt like pressure. A team on a nine-game losing streak facing a division opponent. And instead flattened it into irrelevance. Sometimes that's enough to reset. Sometimes a 15-1 night feels like air after that kind of stretch.",
